.he "chmod (1)                    8/16/26                    chmod (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
chmod
- change mode
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
chmod [ -Rf ] mode file ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Chmod changes the mode of each file.  The mode may be absolute or
symbolic.  An absolute mode is an octal number made by ORing the bits
below.
.sp
.in +10
.ti -10
4000	set user id on execution

.ti -10
2000	set group id on execution

.ti -10
1000	sticky bit

.ti -10
0400	read by owner

.ti -10
0200	write by owner

.ti -10
0100	execute, or search a directory, by owner

.ti -10
0070	read, write and execute by group

.ti -10
0007	read, write and execute by others

.in -10
.sp
A symbolic mode is one or more clauses separated by commas, each of
the form who op permission.  who is any of u, g and o for user, group
and other, or a for all three; left out, it means a.  op is + to add
permission, - to take it away, or = to set the mode exactly, clearing
the other bits.  permission is any of r, w and x, X to add execute
only if the file is a directory or already executable by someone, s
to set the set-id bit, and t to set the sticky bit.  A permission of
u, g or o copies the corresponding bits from the current mode.  The
clauses are worked left to right.  The letter s is only useful with
u or g.
.sp
Only the owner of a file, or the super-user, may change its mode.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-R	Change a directory and everything under it, recursively.

.ti -10
-f	Do not complain about a file whose mode cannot be changed.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The Berkeley chmod, when who was left out, let the umask trim the
bits set by +; micronix has no umask, so a clause with no who acts on
all three classes outright, as it did in v7.
.sp
Micronix has no symbolic links, so -R has nothing to skip.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ls (1), chgrp (1), find (1)
